--TEST--
`Core\Db::open` over the SQLite arm of `Db\Settings` asks `fs.read` about the path and never `db.open`, which is about hosts — `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`, `rule:core-api/shape-parameter`, `rule:core-api/shape-arms-are-disjoint` and `rule:core-api/shape-flattens-at-the-abi`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities` gives a SQLite file the file rule alone:
// `fs.read`, and `fs.write` for a connection that writes. This run's
// configuration grants a `db.open` host and no file, so the open throws naming
// `fs.read` and the path. A `db.open` grant does not open a local file.
try {
    var $db = Core\Db::open({driver: Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite, path: "secrets.db"});
    echo "opened", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`: a denial is the world refusing a call, not the program dying.
echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A host grant, which a SQLite path never asks, and no file at all: a CLI run
# reads every file unless `read` is written.
[capabilities.db]
open = ["*.tenants.internal"]

[capabilities.fs]
read = []
--EXPECTF--
Core\Db::open needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%esecrets.db, which is not granted
help: add `read = ["%s"]` under `[capabilities.fs]` in nvs.toml
still running
